# Grist Core (`gristlabs-grist-core`)

Grist is a modern relational spreadsheet server: it gives you a familiar spreadsheet UI, but with database-style tables and typed columns, plus formulas that recalculate automatically when data changes. In grist-core you get Python formulas (including standard library support and many Excel-style functions), dashboards with linked widgets (charts, cards, calendars, summary tables), imports/exports (CSV, Excel, Google Drive, and Airtable import), a REST API with an interactive API console, webhooks, access rules down to rows/columns/tables, collaboration features like comments and suggestions, and a portable document format based on SQLite.

As an Eyevinn Open Source Cloud managed service, you can provision Grist without standing up Docker hosts, wiring persistence volumes, or maintaining config and upgrades yourself. You get a ready-to-use Grist endpoint quickly, while still building on the Apache-licensed grist-core codebase and its documented configuration options.

Using Grist on OSC also means it fits alongside your other OSC services: you can treat it as a managed component in your stack, while your team focuses on building documents, APIs, and integrations rather than operating the server and its surrounding infrastructure.

[Source](https://github.com/gristlabs/grist-core)

## Example

```terraform
resource "osc_instance" "example" {
  service_id = "gristlabs-grist-core"
  name       = "mycore"

  parameters = {
    S3Endpoint = "..."
    S3Bucket   = "..."
    AdminEmail = "..."
  }

  sensitive_parameters = {
    DatabaseUrl = var.database_url
    S3AccessKey = var.s3_access_key
    S3SecretKey = var.s3_secret_key
    SessionSecret = var.session_secret
  }
}
```

Commented lines are optional parameters. Values marked `"..."` need a real value. Outputs are `url`, `external_ip`, `external_port` and the raw `instance` document, see the `osc_instance` resource.

## Parameters

| Name | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `DatabaseUrl` | string | yes | PostgreSQL connection URL for Grist's home database (users, workspaces, document metadata), for example postgres://user:password@host:5432/grist. Only postgres:// or postgresql:// URLs work; the port defaults to 5432, the database name to grist, and any sslmode other than disable turns on SSL. Feeds DATABASE_URL, which the OSC entrypoint (fork only) splits into the TYPEORM_* variables. Sensitive, set it in `sensitive_parameters`. |
| `S3Endpoint` | string | yes | Full URL of the S3-compatible object storage (for example OSC MinIO) that holds the Grist document files, such as https://minio.example.com or http://host:9000. The port defaults to 443 for https and 80 for http. Feeds S3_ENDPOINT, which the OSC entrypoint (fork only) splits into GRIST_DOCS_S3_ENDPOINT, port and SSL. |
| `S3Bucket` | string | yes | Name of an existing bucket for Grist documents. Versioning must be enabled on the bucket, otherwise Grist stops at startup with a fatal error. Feeds S3_BUCKET (GRIST_DOCS_S3_BUCKET). |
| `S3AccessKey` | string | yes | Access key (user name) for the S3 bucket. For OSC MinIO this is the RootUser. Feeds S3_ACCESS_KEY (GRIST_DOCS_S3_ACCESS_KEY). Sensitive, set it in `sensitive_parameters`. |
| `S3SecretKey` | string | yes | Secret key (password) for the S3 bucket. For OSC MinIO this is the RootPassword. Feeds S3_SECRET_KEY (GRIST_DOCS_S3_SECRET_KEY). Sensitive, set it in `sensitive_parameters`. |
| `AdminEmail` | string | yes | Email address of the Grist default user and installation admin, for example admin@example.com. Open /login once to sign in as this user. Feeds ADMIN_EMAIL, which the OSC entrypoint (fork only) maps to GRIST_DEFAULT_EMAIL and GRIST_ADMIN_EMAIL. |
| `SessionSecret` | string | no | Optional random string used to sign login sessions. If left empty the OSC entrypoint (fork only) generates a new one on each start, which signs everyone out on restart. Feeds GRIST_SESSION_SECRET. Sensitive, set it in `sensitive_parameters`. |

The instance `name` must match `^\w+$` and be unique per service within the workspace. OSC lowercases it and allows at most 20 characters.

Parameter values are strings; booleans take `"true"` or `"false"`. Reference an `osc_secret` as `osc_secret.<name>.ref` to keep a value out of the instance configuration. This page is generated from the OSC catalog; the provider validates against the live catalog when planning.
